#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189102} :activity/id #uuid "74e9671b-54e8-50a3-bb7e-2959c8675a46" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048382 :topic/id #uuid "9aa3613b-2082-5f1b-a095-b04ef602af6c" :topic/math-academy-id 3859} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188278 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188213 :question/id #uuid "c1eefedf-bc2e-5c6c-84e8-0c80aee4a4cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-9267"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d240e02-c926-5dcc-9494-315fdd662109" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188562}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables In Context"} :step/id #uuid "67ca45ff-80bb-5b36-af8f-70150761b296" :step/math-academy-id 34668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189101 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188289 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187993 :question/id #uuid "f8b96980-910c-5e33-b6bf-e1f8532dcdbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10060"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fe2b95-0c68-58f7-a87a-5e4ad3d18e26" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049134}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188560}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "08bccdd5-251e-5183-a107-29a4c922e170" :step/math-academy-id 37958 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189373 :tutorial/content "A set of random variables is said to be **independent and identically distributed** (or **I.I.D.**) if the random variables are mutually independent and have the same probability distribution.\n\nWhen we conduct a random sample of size $n$ from a population, the sample elements\n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad X_n\n$$\n\nare often considered to be I.I.D. random variables. This assumption often allows us to simplify the statistical analysis.\n\nSuppose that the random variables $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are I.I.D, where\n\n$$\nX_i\\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq n\n$$\n\nLet's consider their sum\n\n$$\nY = X_1+X_2+\\cdots + X_n\n$$\n\nSince each $X_i$ is normally distributed, it follows that $Y$ is normally distributed as well. Moreover, we can compute the mean and the variance of $Y$ as follows:\n\n- Calculating the mean using the properties of expectation, we get\n$E[Y]|= E[X_{1} + X_{2} + ⋯ + X_{n}]; = E[X_{1}] + E[X_{2}] + ⋯ + E[X_{n}]; = μ + μ + ⋯ + μ; = nμ$.\n- Similarly, using the properties of variance, we get\n$Var[Y]|= Var[X_{1} + X_{2} + ⋯ + X_{n}]; = Var[X_{1}] + Var[X_{2}] + ⋯ + Var[X_{n}]; = σ^{2} + σ^{2} + ⋯ + σ^{2}; = nσ^{2}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY = X_1+X_2+\\cdots+X_n\\sim N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nWe will soon use this important result to develop models of how random samples are distributed. But for now, let's practice working with I.I.D normal random variables." :tutorial/id #uuid "6bb107aa-ee09-57fa-9b9a-f4267b123dac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6260 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "791c1871-8fb1-51ca-a60a-df0bdf72b5f4" :step/math-academy-id 37959 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189101}}] :activity/title "I.I.D Normal Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188454}]}
